#{[#edb/ref 17592186048445 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116120} :activity/id #uuid "3b658871-23f8-5b8d-92ee-5294fec9bba3" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116120 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116493 :tutorial/content "We can use Gaussian elimination to find the general solutions of systems with any number of equations and variables. For instance, consider the system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x-y+z=1, \\\\ y+3z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis system has the augmented matrix\n![](images/8a/8a8452587098eed9b697c3999c356359b949429f29ce16af2c81cf728be9480e.svg)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\, & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x+4z=1 \\\\ y+3z=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that the basic variables are $x$ and $y,$ and we have one free variable $z.$ From the second equation, we get $y=-3z,$ and from the first equation, we obtain $x=1-4z.$ Therefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=1-4z, \\\\ y=-3z, \\\\ z=z.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1-4z \\\\ -3z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+z[\\begin{aligned}-4 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,z\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116121}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116121 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115241 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115070 :question/id #uuid "dc6dd9b4-de7f-52d0-8ff9-a7a7f7a9abd7" :question/math-academy-id "e-4711" :question/problem "Consider the system of linear equations\n![](images/40/406033f059dc4438ea55d43d2bd00b3b79bb99365a2e4846c995f4a447acfd82.svg)\nUse the standard Gauss-Jordan method to reduce the augmented matrix $M$ of the system to reduced row echelon form, and then solve the linear system." :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](images/be/bed2784d17ee63b805917b492052daa7ef079c1682bbc78468eb10f314a92b1f.svg)\n\nUsing the standard Gauss-Jordan method, we can reduce this matrix to reduced row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 5 & 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-1){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 5 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1 & 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now\n![](images/91/91175ab97472067cd14ffff740bb3a98718436f1e94de7a4a6d7fdfb1b35b81f.svg)\n\nThe basic variables are the variables whose coefficients are pivots. In this case, the basic variables are $x$ and $z,$ and we have one free variable, $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ and $z$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, the general solution is\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1-5y \\\\ y=y \\\\ z=3,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $y$ (the free variable) is an arbitrary number.\n\nIn vector form, the solution can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-1-5y \\\\ y \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}],\\,y\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving a Consistent System of Equations Using Gauss-Jordan Elimination"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116122}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116122 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116494 :tutorial/content "Recall that a system of linear equations is called **consistent** if it has at least one solution. Otherwise, if there are no solutions, the system is called **inconsistent**. \n\nFor a consistent system, \n\n- the system is called **consistent independent** if the solution is unique, while\n\n- the system is called **consistent dependent** if there are infinitely many solutions.\n\n![](images/f5/f5b808eac6e1d865b13b68ce2a4f7d317560ae3965928e478531fcd2148c2c6b.png)" :tutorial/title "Consistent and Inconsistent Systems of Linear Equations"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116209}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116123 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115242 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115071 :question/id #uuid "f2c7cfd3-da43-5208-bd0e-21692357fabe" :question/math-academy-id "e-4712" :question/problem "Determine the value of $k$ for which the following system of linear equations is inconsistent:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x-2y=1, \\\\ 4x+ky=-3\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/worked-solution "The augmented matrix for this system is\n\n![](images/6f/6fa4508463b059ce8ef9a5cfa720de9463e41ecdbfb16f4460870bb6fce444bc.svg)\n\nUsing the method of Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 4 & k & -3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}-2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & k+4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe corresponding system is now \n\n![](images/21/215ff061dc7584055bf607f48d0048cb6bde4dcb1ed028fbf4c3a4bd47d89c25.svg)\n\nThe system is inconsistent if it has no solutions. For the system to have no solutions, one of the equations above must be false.\n\nIf $k=-4,$ then the second equation is $0=-5,$ which is false, and the system will have no solution.\n\nTherefore, our system is inconsistent when $k=-4.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Value of a Constant for Which a Given Linear System Is Consistent or Inconsistent"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116209 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115299 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115124 :question/id #uuid "cd56314d-f45a-55be-9762-983fe507a1a0" :question/math-academy-id "e-6173" :question/problem "Given the system\n![](images/0e/0e89fc57b6af2ddf2a71246dbadc7feb8425d1e1e0ab8f988e7e8ad97d22cff7.svg)\nwhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- The system is consistent dependent\n\n- The system has no solution\n\n- The system is consistent independent" :question/worked-solution "To classify the system, we first need to find its solution. The augmented matrix for this system is\n![](images/3e/3e3e8adb0d6e13f7d3b4c3e450b25a789480ac53189ab92385174688e460b2cc.svg)\n\nUsing Gaussian elimination, we can reduce this matrix to row echelon form as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ -4 & 6 & -8\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+2{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe system is now\n![](images/41/4122fb4ef56aa99161b15bc25001ca728a6cf7dae36d5dac0aac9b03dc411685.svg)\n\nSince any numbers $x$ and $y$ form a solution of the final equation $0x+0y=0,$ we can remove this equation from the system. So, our system now consists of just a single equation:\n\n$$\n2x -3y= 4\n$$\n\nThe basic variable is $x,$ and we have a free variable $y.$ That means we need to find $x$ in terms of $y.$ \n\nTherefore, since there are infinitely many solutions, we conclude that the system is *consistent dependent*.\n\nAs a result, only statement I is true."} :knowledge-point/title "Classifying a System as Consistent Dependent, Consistent Independent, or Inconsistent"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186116123}}] :activity/title "Gaussian Elimination For NxM Systems of Equations" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186115479}] [#edb/ref 17592186048418 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120572} :activity/id #uuid "a165e965-4bdc-5070-bd4a-beeb5a8e6ae9" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120572 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120999 :tutorial/content "For a curve defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=x(t), \\quad y=y(t), \\qquad t\\in [a,b],\n$$\n\nthe arc length of the curve between $t=a$ and $t=b$ is given by\n\n$$\nL = \\int_{a}^{b} \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2}\\,\\textrm{d}t.\n$$\n\nNow, if we express our curve as a vector-valued function $\\mathbf r(t)$ as\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t) = \\left\\langle x(t), y(t)\\right\\rangle, \\qquad t\\in [a,b],\n$$\n\nwe can express the arc length of the curve in a more compact form as follows:\n\n$$\nL= \\int_{a}^{b} \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm d t\n$$\n\nIt's easy to see that this is equivalent to the original arc length formula. First, note that\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r'(t) = \\left\\langle \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}, \\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right\\rangle.\n$$\n\nCalculating the magnitude of this vector, we get\n\n$$\n\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\| =  \\sqrt{\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2+\\left(\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t}\\right)^2},\n$$\n\nand thus the integral of $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|$ with respect to $t$ over $[a,b]$ gives the arc length of the curve.\n\nFinally, note that we can use this arc length formula to find a curve's length in two and three dimensions." :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120912}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120573 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119743 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119559 :question/id #uuid "98c98a56-ca57-5dc6-9dc4-c40da53806f7" :question/math-academy-id "e-3063" :question/problem "Write down the integral that gives the arc length of the curve  $\\mathbf f (t) = \\langle 3 \\sin t, \\: 2\\sin 2t \\rangle$\nfrom $t =\\dfrac {3\\pi} 2$  to $t = 2\\pi.$\n\n![](images/57/574a16e843a9c81e5fc9ddbbe2c0dc2c308ffcac4991bcbf48c7f45b4871c93b.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf f'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf f'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{f}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(3\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡2t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{f}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3\\pi /2}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{9{cos}^{2}⁡t+16{cos}^{2}⁡2t}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Expression For the Arc Length of a Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120574}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120574 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119744 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119560 :question/id #uuid "8932c22b-465f-5a48-962c-e53730e5ba40" :question/math-academy-id "e-3064" :question/problem "Find the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf g (t) = \\langle 3 - 2 \\sin 2t, \\: 1 - 2\\cos 2t \\rangle$ from $t=\\dfrac \\pi 3$ to $\\pi.$\n\n![](images/29/29c97e5963d451cc389944ef113764b2d15998448e773a02bb98c062207c2860.png)" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf g'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf g'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{g}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(3-2\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(1-2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t,\\,4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t⟩ \\\\ ‖{g}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡2t)^{2}+(4\\operatorname{sin}⁡2t)^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16{cos}^{2}⁡2t+16{sin}^{2}⁡2t} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{16} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}‖{g}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi }4\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =4t\\,{|}_{\\pi /3}^{\\pi } \\\\  & =4(\\pi -\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{8\\pi }{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Planar Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120759}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120575 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121000 :tutorial/content "Given a curve $C$ parametrized by a function $\\mathbf{r}(t)$, we can approximate the arc length by breaking up the curve into many small line segments and adding up their lengths.\n\nTo do this, we first choose a finite number of points $P_0,$ $P_1,$ $\\cdots,$ $P_n$ along $C$ and form the polygonal path\n\n$$\n\\overline{P_0P_1} \\cup \\overline{P_1P_2} \\cup \\cdots \\cup \\overline{P_{n-1}P_n},\n$$\n\nas shown in the diagram below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d320e6a0f63a823e049203203cbc13c210e80bc98aab5a53bcb4e5299240920.png)\n\nThen, we can approximate the arc length as the sum of the lengths of the line segments:\n\n$$\nL \\approx \\, \\big\\| \\overline{P_0P_1} \\big\\| + \\big\\| \\overline{P_1P_2} \\big\\| + \\ldots + \\big\\| \\overline{P_{n-1}P_n} \\big\\|.\n$$\n\nIf $P_k$ is at the tip of $\\mathbf r(t_k),$ then by the mean value theorem,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{k-1}{P}_{k}}‖ & =‖{r}^{'}({c}_{k})‖({t}_{k}-{t}_{k-1}) \\\\  & =‖{r}^{'}({c}_{k})‖Δ{t}_{k}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nfor some intermediate point $c_k \\in [t_{k-1}, t_k],$ where $\\Delta t_k = t_k - t_{k-1}.$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & \\approx \\,‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{0}{P}_{1}}‖+‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{1}{P}_{2}}‖+…+‖\\overset{―}{{P}_{n-1}{P}_{n}}‖ \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{n}‖{r}^{'}({c}_{k})‖Δ{t}_{k}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis is a Riemann sum, and the closer the points $P_k$ are to each other, the better our approximation will be. So, in the limit as $n\\to\\infty$ (or $\\Delta t_k \\to 0),$ we have\n\n$$\nL = \\lim_{n\\to\\infty}\\sum_{k=1}^{n}  \\| \\mathbf r'(c_k) \\| \\Delta t_k=\\int_{a}^{b} \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$" :tutorial/title "Deriving the Arc Length Formula"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120759 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119860 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119675 :question/id #uuid "118ad24e-627f-51ff-9b9c-9319c50956f9" :question/math-academy-id "e-6303" :question/problem "![](images/85/8573aedcd26cba038b2db072560cd3d6ba7676542888a2d9b8a356713eaf6724.png)\n\nFind the arc length of the curve $\\mathbf r (t) = \\langle \\sin t ,\\: \\cos t, \\: t \\rangle$\nfrom  $t =0$ to $t = 2\\pi.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find $\\mathbf r'(t)$ and $\\|\\mathbf r'(t)\\|\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{′}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(t)⟩ \\\\  & =⟨\\operatorname{cos}⁡t,\\,-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,1⟩ \\\\ ‖{r}^{′}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡t)^{2}+(-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t)^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{cos}^{2}⁡t+{sin}^{2}⁡t+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the arc length of the curve is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }‖{r}^{′}(t)‖\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }\\sqrt{2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\text{ }\\sqrt{2}\\,t{|}_{0}^{2\\pi } \\\\  & =\\sqrt{2}(2\\pi -0) \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{2}\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Arc Length of a Three-Dimensional Spatial Curve"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120575}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120912 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186121134 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to calculate the arc length of the curve $C$ given by the vector-valued function\n\n$$\n\\mathbf r(t)= \\left\\langle \\dfrac{t^3}{3}, \\: \\dfrac{t^2}{2}\\right\\rangle, \\qquad    t \\in [0,1].\n$$\n\n \n\nThe length of this curve is given by\n\n$$\nL= \\int_{0}^{1} \\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\| \\, \\textrm d t.\n$$\n\nTo compute $\\| \\mathbf r'(t) \\|,$ we take the derivative of $\\mathbf r(t)$ and compute its magnitude as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{r}^{'}(t) & =⟨\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{3}}{3}),\\,\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{{t}^{2}}{2})⟩ \\\\  & =⟨{t}^{2},\\,t⟩, \\\\ ‖{r}^{'}(t)‖ & =\\sqrt{({t}^{2})^{2}+{t}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the curve $C$ is given by the integral\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{4}+{t}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}\\cdot ({t}^{2}+1)}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{{t}^{2}}\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}|t|\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can solve this integral using substitution. Let $u = t^2+1,$ then $\\textrm d u = 2 t\\,\\textrm d t.$ Therefore, our integral becomes\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}t\\cdot \\sqrt{{t}^{2}+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}\\sqrt{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{1}^{2}{u}^{1/2}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{3/2}]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(2\\sqrt{2}-1).\\end{aligned}\n$$" :tutorial/title "A Concrete Example"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120573}}] :activity/title "The Arc Length of a Vector-Valued Function" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186120036}] [#edb/ref 17592186048423 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158376} :activity/id #uuid "986c9303-5c90-5d19-85f8-915c37c8bfb0" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158376 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158530 :tutorial/content "Suppose we want to find all of the solutions to the equation\n\n$$\nx \\cdot 3^x - 2 \\cdot 3^x = 0.\n$$\n\nFirst, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x\\cdot {3}^{x}-2\\cdot {3}^{x} & =0 \\\\ (x-2)\\cdot {3}^{x} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx-2  = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^x=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x-2=0$ has the solution $x=2.$\n\n- The equation $3^x=0$ has no real solution because $3^x$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has a single solution, $x=2.$" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158377}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158377 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157437 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157170 :question/id #uuid "d6fce272-8b91-53f1-bfa4-32f098d8ee86" :question/math-academy-id "e-7836" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $x^2e^{x-1} - 2x e^{x-1} -15 e^{x-1}  = 0?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}{e}^{x-1}-2x{e}^{x-1}-15{e}^{x-1} & =0 \\\\ ({x}^{2}-2x-15){e}^{x-1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\nx^2 - 2x - 15 = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad e^{x-1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $x^2 - 2x - 15  = 0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(x+3)(x-5)=0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-3,5.$\n\n- The equation $e^{x-1}=0$ has no solutions because $e^{x-1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-3,5,$ and their product is $(-3) \\cdot 5 = -15.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Factoring"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158378}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158378 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157438 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157171 :question/id #uuid "f1a4b9b1-a6fd-504b-882f-df32959c35ee" :question/math-academy-id "e-7838" :question/problem "What is the product of solutions of the equation $9x^2\\cdot 3^{x+1} = 16\\cdot 3^{x+1}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we factor the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} \\\\ 9{x}^{2}\\cdot {3}^{x+1}-16\\cdot {3}^{x+1} & =0 \\\\ (9{x}^{2}-16){3}^{x+1} & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now apply the zero product property, which states that $ab=0$ if and only if $a=0$ or $b=0.$ This gives us two equations,\n\n$$\n9x^2 - 16  = 0 \\quad \\textrm{and} \\quad 3^{x+1}=0.\n$$\n\nWe now solve each of these equations separately.\n\n- The equation $9x^2 - 16=0$ can be factored as\n\n$$\n(3x+4)(3x-4) = 0,\n$$\n\nwhich leads to the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\n- The equation $3^{x+1}=0$ has no solutions because $3^{x+1}$ is positive for every real number $x.$\n\nTherefore, the original equation has the solutions $x=-\\dfrac{4}{3},\\dfrac{4}{3},$ and their product is $\\left( -\\dfrac{4}{3} \\right) \\cdot \\dfrac{4}{3} = -\\dfrac{16}{9}.$"} :knowledge-point/title "Solving an Exponential Equation by Rearranging the Equation and Then Factoring"}}] :activity/title "Solving Exponential Equations Using the Zero-Product Property" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186157527}] [#edb/ref 17592186048489 {:activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172906} :activity/id #uuid "3b336b02-2871-5881-b0a6-2eb5ee14cff9" :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172903 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172038 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171844 :question/id #uuid "231de5e4-8c55-5129-baaf-57673050ddbc" :question/math-academy-id "e-8505" :question/problem "For the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 2x^2-y,\n$$\n\nplot the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$" :question/worked-solution "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(1,1),$ $(0,1),$ and $(1,2).$\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,1)} & =2(1)^{2}-1=1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(0,1)} & =2(0)^{2}-1=-1 \\\\ {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)} & =2(1)^{2}-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](images/0c/0c06b4acee1445fea91bcbb7bed5a837a1dd494c9638a4f8945f5ac8dc226c22.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172904}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172904 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172039 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171845 :question/id #uuid "0444de80-19f1-5251-b159-5dc55756bb19" :question/math-academy-id "e-8506" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x} = -2xy$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point in the second quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n\n- At every point in the third quadrant, the slope of the slope field is negative\n\n- At every point in the fourth quadrant, the slope of the slope field is positive" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y) = -2xy.$ Then, our differential equation is $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y) = 0$ when $x=0$ or $y=0.$ The lines $x=0$ and $y=0$ divide the plane into the usual four quadrants.\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each quadrant (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/2c/2c218c7df2bf4e159b4bffaccb4296beab8e3f44ab82d56b3589050af92ccf8e.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is false. If $(x,y)$ is in the second quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the second quadrant.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the third quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y)  \\lt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in the third quadrant.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $(x,y)$ is in the fourth quadrant, then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} = f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in the fourth quadrant.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Quadrants Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172907}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172905 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172040 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171846 :question/id #uuid "3602a14d-6bd7-5586-a6e8-51d069dc8641" :question/math-academy-id "e-8507" :question/problem "Which of the following could be the slope field for the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = x+y?$ \n\n![](images/3b/3bd19865d2a1bb6bbb40c57112f269b51a3740d01da2c80b81db4eb84c6766f1.png)" :question/worked-solution "Notice that we are given an equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).\n$$\n\nThe right-hand side is a function of $x$ and $y.$ This means that the slope varies with both $x$ and $y.$\n\nOf the given slope fields, the only one that varies with both $x$ and $y$ is the following:\n\n![](images/54/543bb98ff948d1186e05d84db7992f9d5033ba4eaf47a9787b6b5664b88510a2.png)\n\n \n\nTo see this more clearly, notice that if we draw a vertical line and a horizontal line, the slopes vary if we move along either of the two lines.\n\n![](images/43/433f0cf3835465e7a0cca195f8b0f0b280984200d84f49533219f08967adc497.png)"} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Slope Field Based on the Type of Equation"}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172906 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173161 :tutorial/content "Let's plot the slope field of the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac {\\textrm{d}y} {\\textrm{d}x}=x-y.\n$$\n\nNotice that the right-hand side of our equation is a function of both $x$ and $y.$ However, the procedure for sketching the slope field is the same as usual.\n\nWe start by setting up a table of values.\n\n|  | $x=-1$ | $x=0$ | $x=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=1$ |  |  |  |\n| $y=0$ |  |  |  |\n| $y=-1$ |  |  |  |\n\nWe then calculate the slope at each point and record each answer in the table. For example, the slope at the point $(0,1)$ is\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x}\\right|_{(0,1)} = 0-1 ={\\color{blue}{-1}}.\n$$\n\nFilling in our table gives the following set of results:\n\n\\[\n\\begin{array}{|c|c|c|c|c|c|}\n\\hline\n\t&-1 \t&0 \t&1 \\\\\n\\hline\n -1\t&0 \t&1 \t&2\\\\\n\\hline\n0\t&-1 \t&0 \t&1\\\\\n\\hline\n1\t&-2 \t&{\\color{blue}{-1}} \t&0\\\\\n\\hline\n\\end{array}\n\\]\n\n|  | $x=-1$ | $x=0$ | $x=1$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y=1$ | $-2$ | ${\\color{blue}{-1}}$ | $0$ |\n| $y=0$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ |\n| $y=-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n\nFinally, sketching our slope field, we get the following diagram:\n\n![](images/2a/2a86de3bdb5716e2be58f02124ccfd7470c9d8eda50abdb01052200e9fecfa0d.png)\n\nWe're done!\n\nNote that if we were to pick more points and use a computer to plot our slope field, it would look more like this:\n\n![](images/42/42508880923b52e9a63b90514c01b33b68c45385cb1650e44832953a6111ee7b.png)\n\nFrom here, it's easy to get an idea of the solution curves. For example, three particular solutions to the differential equation are given below.\n\n![](images/76/7634837eabf2be5ceb2ac56d22576170238b2640181aadde2417e8ff7a2bbd83.png)" :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172903}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172907 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172041 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186171847 :question/id #uuid "ab9fe3df-7f60-55c5-b8af-810b0746993e" :question/math-academy-id "e-8576" :question/problem "Consider the differential equation $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}x}=(x+1)(y+1)$ and its slope field. Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is positive\n\n- At every point $(x,y)$ where $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ the slope of the slope field is negative" :question/worked-solution "First, we define $f(x,y) = (x+1)(y+1).$ Then, our differential equation is $\\dfrac{\\textrm dy}{\\textrm d x} = f(x,y).$ \n\nNotice that $f(x,y) = 0$ when $x = -1$ or $y = -1.$ This divides the $xy$-plane into four regions, as shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e7b398c7ff971e39b15980078232556d23866ab2f65a05b7d8df9a5f67b797dc.png)\n\nIf we compute the sign of $f(x,y)$ in each of those four regions (by considering some test points, for example), we get the following:\n\n![](images/94/943ed7c12c71438d567da5ec901435bae1d3c7e576ba9b6c933d9fddc4e42b8f.png)\n\nWith that in mind, let's check each statement:\n\n- Statement I is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\gt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement II is true. If $x \\lt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\gt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is positive for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\n- Statement III is true. If $x \\gt -1$ and $y \\lt -1,$ then according to our diagram, we have\n\n$$\n\\left.\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} \\right|_{(x,y)} =f(x,y)  \\lt     0.\n$$\n\nThis implies that the slope of the slope field is negative for any point $(x, y)$ in this region.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Rectangular Regions Where a Slope Field Is Positive or Negative"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172978}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172978 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186173194 :tutorial/content "Note the following facts regarding slope fields:\n\n- Recall that for a differential equation of the form $y' = f(x),$ the slope field has *vertical* symmetry.\n\n- Also recall that for a differential equation of the form $y' = g(y),$ the slope field has *horizontal* symmetry.\n\n- In contrast, a differential equation of the form $y' = f(x,y)$ usually has neither vertical nor horizontal symmetry.\n\nWe can use these facts to determine the type of equation that generates a particular slope field. Let's see an example." :tutorial/title "Symmetry in Slope Fields"} :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186172905}}] :activity/title "Slope Fields for Nonautonomous Differential Equations" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186172174}]}
